    "C68","C69","C73","C77"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "In fasted healthy subjects, propranolol—a lipophilic basic secondary amine with pKa near 9.5—has absolute oral bioavailability 27.2%, above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C10","C25"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Metoprolol, a lipophilic basic secondary amine with a methoxyphenyl group, shows systemic availability 84% in cirrhosis, supporting high F for this structural class.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C15","C22","C30","C31"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Diphenhydramine has systemic availability 0.69 after oral dosing in fasted healthy volunteers.",
      "card_ids": ["C13","C33"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Verapamil, a lipophilic basic amine with dimethoxyphenyl, has absolute F 52.3% in cirrhosis and the lowest visible record is 22%, still above 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C05","C06","C19","C43","C35"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Human absorption records show near-complete absorption for similar basic amines (propranolol >90%, metoprolol ~100%, alprenolol 93-96%).",
      "card_ids": ["C64","C60","C63","C61","C58"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Caco-2 bidirectional assays show propranolol has high symmetric permeability (A-B 40.9 and B-A 41.5, or 33.7 and 33.5 at 200 µM), indicating high passive diffusion without efflux.",
      "card_ids": ["C68","C69","C73","C77"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance records show high intrinsic clearance for some lipophilic basic amines (e.g., HLM Clint 186-223 µl/min/mg for chlorphenoxamine-like compounds), indicating possible first-pass loss, but these are low-transferability and not threshold-specific; propranolol with high first-pass still has F 27.2%.",
      "card_ids": ["C85","C88","C83","C90","C10","C25"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C85","C88","C83","C90"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule.",
    "L6 hepatic clearance records are for reference molecules, not the query; transferability is limited by structural differences and low transfer likelihood.",
    "High intrinsic clearance is not threshold-specific; it does not establish F <20%.",
    "Most high-F analog records come from cirrhosis or modified-release conditions; fasted healthy analogs are limited to propranolol and diphenhydramine.",
    "The query's very low neutral fraction (0.01) at pH 7.4 is not directly tested, though propranolol with similar pKa shows high symmetric permeability."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high because all visible direct F records for structurally similar lipophilic basic amines are >=22% (propranolol 27.2% fasted, diphenhydramine 69% fasted, metoprolol 84% cirrhosis, verapamil 22-52%), L4 shows near-complete human absorption for this class, and L5 Caco-2 data show high symmetric permeability without efflux. L6 high intrinsic clearance records introduce a low-transferability first-pass concern but do not affirm F <20%; propranolol with high first-pass still has F 27.2%. Confidence remains moderate."
}

